Right now, if there's a tie for the highest bid, the winner is essentially chosen arbitrarily.

Here's the current logic in the `onbidwrite` function that finds the winner:

```typescript
// 4. Find the winning bid
let winnerId = '';
let highestBid = -1;
for (const userId in bids) {
  if (bids[userId] > highestBid) {
    highestBid = bids[userId];
    winnerId = userId;
  }
}
```

Because of the `>` (greater than) check, the `winnerId` is only updated when a new bid is strictly higher than the previous `highestBid`. If two users bid the same highest amount, the winner will be whichever of those two users happens to be processed first in the `for...in` loop. The order of keys in a JavaScript object is not guaranteed, so it's effectively random and unpredictable.

This is not ideal. A better approach would be to detect a tie and handle it explicitly, for example, by starting a new, smaller bidding round just for the tied users.

